  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1106 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern continues to be headlined by a large upper-\nlevel high over the southeastern CONUS. A very deep low pressure \nsystem has developed off the coast of the far Pacific Northwest. \nOnly very light showers formed this afternoon before quickly \ndissipating despite the surface-level weak low pressure system \ncontinuing to reside in far western Kansas. \n\nThursday has continued to look like a repeat of Wednesday. In \naddition to the very hot highs near 100 degrees (and even up to 105 \ndegrees), the precipitation potential also is reminiscent. With \nstorms not panning out Wednesday, CAMs are showing little to no \nsignal for Thursday. Again like Wednesday, some weak pop-up showers \nare possible in far west Kansas but these chances remain <10%. \nApparent temperatures for Thursday are right around the criteria for \na Heat Advisory in the far eastern counties, but with it being \nmarginal the headline decision will be punted to the morning \nforecast cycle. \n\nFriday and Saturday will continue to be very hot days with highs up \nto and exceeding the triple digit barrier. Friday will similarly \napproach criteria, but is currently forecast to remain just shy of \nthe 105 apparent temperature mark. CAMs for both days show chances \nfor storms in far western Kansas on both late afternoons. Ensembles \nplace these chances at around 30-40%, but the isolated nature of the \nstorms may make the tangible chances feel lower. Laughably low shear \nis forecast to keep storms from intensifying on Friday. Saturday's \nconvection is forecast to remain sub-severe, but with more shear and \nCAPE (30 KTs and 1200 J/kg from NAMNST forecast soundings), a very \nmarginal severe storm can not be ruled out. The more likely outcome \nis winds up to 50 mph and penny sized hail. \n\nAfter Saturday, temperatures are forecast to cool a couple degrees. \nIt will still be hot and the difference will be hard to notice but \nnonetheless it will be cooler. By midweek on Wednesday, highs may \nonly reach up to the mid-90s. On Wednesday this continues to bring \nthe best chance for widespread precipitation. While chances are only \nat around 20-30% via ensembles, it is the only notable chances for \nthe eastern half of the CWA to see precipitation in the forecast \nperiod. With so much temporal uncertainty, more time will be needed \nto give better details and a more accurate forecast.",
